                    node(NodeKind.SvgGroup, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "-88"), attr("y", "-24"), attr("width", "176"), attr("height", "48"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                        node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="…", style="sug-value", attributes=[attr("x", "0"), attr("y", "8"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                    ], style="sug-record sug-future", attributes=[attr("data-motion-decorative", flag=true), attr("aria-hidden", "true"), attr("focusable", "false"), attr("style", "--arrival-start:9200ms;offset-path:path('M-100 248 H616 Q640 248 640 272 V408 Q640 432 664 432 H840')")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgGroup, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "-88"), attr("y", "-24"), attr("width", "176"), attr("height", "48"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                        node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="…", style="sug-value", attributes=[attr("x", "0"), attr("y", "8"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                    ], style="sug-record sug-future", attributes=[attr("data-motion-decorative", flag=true), attr("aria-hidden", "true"), attr("focusable", "false"), attr("style", "--arrival-start:11300ms;offset-path:path('M-100 248 H616 Q640 248 640 272 V408 Q640 432 664 432 H840')")]),
                ], attributes=[attr("clip-path", "url(#sug-record-clip)")]),
            ], attributes=[attr("viewBox", "0 0 1308 452"), attr("role", "img"), attr("aria-labelledby", "sug-title sug-desc")]),
            group([
                paragraph([
                    strong("Each arriving record becomes a new row."),
                    span("The input keeps growing, while the table’s schema stays the same."),
                ]),
            ], style="sug-cues"),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous row", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "prev")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next row", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "next")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Play", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "play"), attr("aria-pressed", "false")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Pause", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "pause"), attr("aria-pressed", "true")]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("type", "button"), attr("data-motion-action", "replay")]),
                span("Space: pause / resume", style="sug-control-hint"),
                span("5 rows shown · stream continues", style="sug-progress", attributes=[attr("data-stream-progress", flag=true)]),
            ], style="sug-controls", attributes=[attr("data-motion-controls", flag=true), attr("hidden", flag=true), attr("role", "group"), attr("aria-label", "Stream animation controls")]),
            node(NodeKind.Paragraph, content="Five illustrated rows in an unbounded input table.", style="sr-only", attributes=[attr("role", "status"), attr("aria-live", "polite"), attr("aria-atomic", "true"), attr("data-motion-status", flag=true)]),
        ], style="sug-figure", attributes=[attr("data-stream-root", flag=true), attr("data-frame", "static"), attr("tabindex", "0"), attr("aria-label", "Growing input table animation")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("80"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Original corpus: Section 8, 8.2 Spark Structured Streaming, slide 7. Presenter narrative: “This is a streaming DataFrame. We can use familiar DataFrame operations, but the input keeps arriving. That brings a few differences, which we will cover through the rest of this section.” In PySpark, readStream creates a streaming DataFrame; DataStream is not a separate object type here. Avoid promising identical behaviour or support for every batch operation. Start empty. The water moves continuously, and text records float in automatically: cat dog, dog dog, owl cat, dog, owl. Each full record becomes one row in the same value column; words have not yet been split. The ellipsis records continue into the open lower end after the first five rows, so the animation does not suggest that the source ends. The canal represents continuing arrivals only, not a network route, partition, executor or buffer implementation. Depicted order and spacing are illustrative, not a DataFrame ordering guarantee or trigger schedule. The later file example groups the first two and last two records into batches. The growing input table is a logical model, not a claim that Spark retains all input history in memory or rereads it each time. Query state and output follow on the next slides. Animation starts on the first visit to this slide. Pause freezes water and records in place; Play resumes, Replay clears the example and restarts, and Previous/Next row lets the presenter inspect arrivals. Leaving the slide pauses it. Reduced-motion, static URLs, print and no-JavaScript views show the complete five-row illustration. Source: https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/getting-started.html and https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_first_query() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-first-query",
        title="Start processing the arriving data",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-first-run",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Start processing the arriving data", level=1),
            group([
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                    code("""query = (
    counts.writeStream
    .format("console")
    .outputMode("complete")
    .start()
)"""),
                ], style="language-python"),
                prose("A small console demonstration using the word-count query we just defined.", style="ss-run-note"),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.DefinitionList, children=[
                group([
                    node(NodeKind.Term, children=[
                        code("counts"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Definition, content="The streaming DataFrame describes the result we want."),
                ]),
                group([
                    node(NodeKind.Term, children=[
                        code(".start()"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Definition, content="Starts executing that query as input arrives."),
                ]),
                group([
                    node(NodeKind.Term, children=[
                        code("query"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                        text("A "),
                        code("StreamingQuery"),
                        text(" handle for monitoring and stopping the run."),
                    ]),
                ]),
            ], style="ss-run-explanation"),
        ], style="ss-run-grid"),
            prose("The query is now running. Next, we’ll look at how Spark processes new input.", style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("81"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Continue the counts definition from the earlier read/readStream example. Defining counts described a query; start() starts execution and returns a StreamingQuery. No trigger is set, so this demonstration uses the default micro-batch schedule. Console is a debugging destination. Complete prints all current counts after each batch. Both choices receive their own explanations later; mention their purpose here without enumerating alternatives. No explicit durable checkpoint is configured in this minimal classroom example, so it is not a restart recipe. The fuller writer configuration later adds the checkpoint path and named memory sink. Stop this demonstration with query.stop() before running that separate example. The code is an explanatory fragment that assumes spark, counts and the prepared input directory exist.

https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.start.html
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/getting-started.html""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_execution_modes() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-execution-modes",
        title="Streaming execution modes",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-execution-overview",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Streaming execution modes", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Three execution modes for a Structured Streaming query", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.Columns, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Column, style="ss-execution-name"),
                node(NodeKind.Column, style="ss-execution-behaviour"),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Mode", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="How input is processed", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="What to keep in mind", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                ]),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Micro-batch"),
                        span("The default"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("Successive portions of new input."),
                        span("Each batch finishes before the next starts."),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("Runs as soon as possible by default."),
                        span("A fixed interval is optional."),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Real-time mode"),
                        span("Records processed as they arrive"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("Long-running tasks pass records through the query."),
                        span("Progress is checkpointed periodically."),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("For low-latency workloads."),
                        span("Supported queries and connectors depend on the Spark version and runtime."),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Continuous Processing"),
                        span("Older · experimental"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("Processes records continuously."),
                        span("A separate engine from Real-time mode."),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("Restricted operations and connectors."),
                        span("No aggregations; at-least-once processing."),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-execution-legacy"),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-execution-table"),
            paragraph([
            strong("Execution mode"),
            text(" controls how the query runs."),
            line_break(),
            strong("Output mode"),
            text(" controls which result rows are sent to the sink."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("82"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""We have a streaming DataFrame. Once we start a query against it, how does Spark process the arriving data? These are alternative execution modes, not consecutive stages. Micro-batch is the default and is what we will use for the word-count example next. Without an explicit trigger interval, Spark starts the next batch as soon as the previous one finishes. A fixed processing-time interval is optional; it is not a completion deadline.

Real-time mode processes records as they arrive within long-running execution, with periodic progress checkpoints. A configured realTime duration is not a wait before each record can be processed. Do not promise microsecond end-to-end latency: per-record processing describes the execution approach, and observed latency also depends on the query, resources, source and sink. PySpark 4.2 exposes the realTime trigger. Query support varies by Spark version and vendor runtime; do not imply that the following stateful word count can run unchanged in every mode. The Databricks source describes that runtime's architecture and is not a universal connector/operator compatibility matrix.

Continuous Processing is the separate, older experimental mode introduced in Spark 2.3. It supports a restricted set of operations such as projections and filters, excludes aggregations and provides at-least-once processing. Keep this brief so older documentation does not confuse it with Real-time mode. AvailableNow is a micro-batch trigger for processing available data and stopping, rather than a fourth execution engine. Execution and output modes are different choices, with compatibility constraints; Append, Update and Complete are output modes.

Sources (checked 20 September 2026): https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#triggers ; https://spark.apache.org/releases/spark-release-4-2-0.html ; https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.trigger.html ; https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/performance-tips.html#continuous-processing ; https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/real-time/concepts""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_programming_model() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-programming-model",
        title="How Spark keeps the result up to date",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-incremental-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("How Spark keeps the result up to date", level=1),
            group([
            group([
                strong("Count each word"),
                span("Micro-batch execution"),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                code("words.groupBy(\"word\").count()"),
            ], style="language-python"),
        ], style="ss-incremental-query"),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="The same word-count query incorporates three batches of new input into its existing counts.", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.Columns, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Column, style="ss-incremental-row-name"),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.Cell),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Batch 1", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Batch 2", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Batch 3", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                ]),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="New input", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Code, children=[
                            text("cat dog"),
                            line_break(),
                            text("dog dog"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        code("owl cat"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Code, children=[
                            text("dog"),
                            line_break(),
                            text("owl"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-incremental-input"),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Running"),
                        line_break(),
                        text("counts"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.DefinitionList, children=[
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="cat"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                                    strong("1"),
                                ]),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="dog"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                                    strong("3"),
                                ]),
                            ]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.DefinitionList, children=[
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="cat"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                                    span("1 + 1 = "),
                                    strong("2"),
                                ]),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="dog"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, content="3"),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="owl"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                                    strong("1"),
                                ]),
                            ]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.DefinitionList, children=[
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="cat"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, content="2"),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="dog"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                                    span("3 + 1 = "),
                                    strong("4"),
                                ]),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                node(NodeKind.Term, content="owl"),
                                node(NodeKind.Definition, children=[
                                    span("1 + 1 = "),
                                    strong("2"),
                                ]),
                            ]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-incremental-result"),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-incremental-table"),
            paragraph([
            text("Spark updates the existing counts with each new batch."),
            line_break(),
            span("When does the next batch start? That depends on the trigger."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("83"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""We now have a DataFrame whose input keeps arriving. What happens when we run a query against it? We define the query once. Once started, Spark keeps processing new input and updating the result.

Use the same cat, dog and owl input lines from the preceding growing-table slide. Here words means the streaming DataFrame after splitting those lines into individual words; the full code follows later. For this illustration, the first two lines arrive in batch 1, owl cat in batch 2, and dog plus owl in batch 3. These batch boundaries are illustrative, not implied by the animation's arrival timing.

Read from left to right. Batch 1 produces cat 1 and dog 3. Batch 2 brings owl cat: Spark adds one to the existing cat count and creates an owl count. The dog count stays at three. Batch 3 adds dog and owl, taking the counts to cat 2, dog 4 and owl 2. Bold green values are newly created or updated; the equations show how the old count contributes. Batch 1 contains only cat and dog: the owl result row first appears when owl arrives in batch 2.

Spark does not need to count the entire input history again. It retains aggregation state and incorporates the next portion of new input. We are showing the logical running result, not every detail of partial aggregation, state-store layout or physical partitioning, and not the rows already written to a sink. This worked example uses default micro-batch execution. The next batch normally starts as soon as the previous one finishes; a fixed interval is optional. Real-time mode has different execution and support constraints, so do not suggest the same word-count query is supported unchanged in every runtime and mode.

We have seen how the counts change. Next, explain when a new micro-batch starts by comparing trigger settings, then introduce Real-time mode. After that, return to this example for the output-mode question: which results should Spark send to the destination?

Sources: https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/getting-started.html ; https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#triggers""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_microbatch_triggers() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-microbatch-triggers",
        title="Micro-batch triggers",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-trigger-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Micro-batch triggers", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Ways to schedule micro-batches, configured on a streaming writer before starting the query", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.Columns, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Column, style="ss-trigger-name"),
                node(NodeKind.Column, style="ss-trigger-setting"),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Trigger", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Setting on "),
                        code("writeStream"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Behaviour", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                ]),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Default", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Span, children=[
                            text("No "),
                            code(".trigger(...)"),
                            text(" setting"),
                        ], style="ss-trigger-default"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Start the next batch as soon as the previous one finishes."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Processing time", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                            code(".trigger(processingTime=\"5 seconds\")"),
                        ], style="language-python"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Every 5 seconds, or as soon as an overrunning batch finishes."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="AvailableNow", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                            code(".trigger(availableNow=True)"),
                        ], style="language-python"),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Process the data available at the start, then stop. May use several batches."),
                ]),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-trigger-table"),
            group([
            strong("Once · deprecated"),
            code(".trigger(once=True)"),
            node(NodeKind.Span, children=[
                text("One batch, then stop."),
                line_break(),
                text("Use AvailableNow for new work."),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-trigger-legacy"),
            prose("The trigger controls the schedule. It does not set a batch’s size or completion deadline.", style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("84"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""We have seen what happens inside successive micro-batches. Now we choose how the next one starts. These are trigger settings within micro-batch execution, not new execution engines. Apply a trigger on the configured writeStream builder before calling start; the source, sink and checkpoint configuration are omitted here so the settings remain easy to compare.

Default: leave the trigger unspecified. The next batch starts as soon as the previous batch completes. Processing time: request a fixed cadence, here five seconds. If the batch finishes early, Spark waits for the next scheduled start. If it overruns, the next batch starts as soon as it finishes; Spark does not launch overlapping batches to catch up. Five seconds is not a guarantee that the work finishes in five seconds.

AvailableNow: process the records available when this run begins, potentially across several batches, and stop. Source admission controls such as maxFilesPerTrigger can limit each batch. With a compatible retained checkpoint, a later run can resume progress, making this useful for scheduled catch-up workloads. Check source support; the documented fallback for unsupported sources is one-time execution. New arrivals after the run's initial target belong to a later run.

Once is included for older code only. It runs one batch and is deprecated in favour of AvailableNow. The trigger alone is not a row-count or byte-size setting: the source and its admission controls determine the work in a batch. Before discussing which results get written, we will look at the other current execution approach: Real-time mode.

Sources (checked 20 September 2026): https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#triggers ; https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.trigger.html""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_real_time() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-real-time",
        title="Real-time mode",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-realtime-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Real-time mode", level=1),
            prose("Long-running tasks process records as they arrive.", style="ss-realtime-lead"),
            node(NodeKind.Figure, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Svg, children=[
                node(NodeKind.SvgTitle, content="Processing records throughout a long-running batch", attributes=[attr("id", "ss-rt-title")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgDescription, content="A stateless Kafka example reads, transforms and writes records throughout one long-running batch. The time line ends at the progress checkpoint boundary. The example duration of five minutes governs that boundary, not a five-minute wait before each record is processed. The three operations may be fused into a single task.", attributes=[attr("id", "ss-rt-desc")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgDefinitions, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgMarker, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgPath, attributes=[attr("d", "M0 0L8 3L0 6Z"), attr("fill", "#67747b")]),
                    ], attributes=[attr("id", "ss-rt-arrow"), attr("markerWidth", "8"), attr("markerHeight", "6"), attr("refX", "7"), attr("refY", "3"), attr("orient", "auto")]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="During one long-running batch", style="ss-rt-heading", attributes=[attr("x", "24"), attr("y", "27")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Example: stateless Kafka → Kafka", style="ss-rt-small", attributes=[attr("x", "1284"), attr("y", "27"), attr("text-anchor", "end")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgPath, style="ss-rt-connector", attributes=[attr("d", "M304 99H482")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgPath, style="ss-rt-connector", attributes=[attr("d", "M824 99H1002")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-rt-endpoint", attributes=[attr("x", "24"), attr("y", "56"), attr("width", "280"), attr("height", "88"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Read records", style="ss-rt-node", attributes=[attr("x", "164"), attr("y", "94"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Kafka input topic", style="ss-rt-small", attributes=[attr("x", "164"), attr("y", "122"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-rt-transform", attributes=[attr("x", "484"), attr("y", "56"), attr("width", "340"), attr("height", "88"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Transform records", style="ss-rt-node", attributes=[attr("x", "654"), attr("y", "94"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="select · filter", style="ss-rt-small", attributes=[attr("x", "654"), attr("y", "122"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-rt-endpoint", attributes=[attr("x", "1004"), attr("y", "56"), attr("width", "280"), attr("height", "88"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Write records", style="ss-rt-node", attributes=[attr("x", "1144"), attr("y", "94"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Kafka output topic", style="ss-rt-small", attributes=[attr("x", "1144"), attr("y", "122"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgLine, style="ss-rt-timeline", attributes=[attr("x1", "24"), attr("y1", "184"), attr("x2", "1284"), attr("y2", "184")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgLine, style="ss-rt-tick", attributes=[attr("x1", "24"), attr("y1", "174"), attr("x2", "24"), attr("y2", "194")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgCircle, style="ss-rt-checkpoint", attributes=[attr("cx", "1284"), attr("cy", "184"), attr("r", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Batch starts", style="ss-rt-small", attributes=[attr("x", "24"), attr("y", "224")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Records are processed throughout", style="ss-rt-small", attributes=[attr("x", "654"), attr("y", "224"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Checkpoint progress", style="ss-rt-boundary", attributes=[attr("x", "1284"), attr("y", "224"), attr("text-anchor", "end")]),
            ], attributes=[attr("viewBox", "0 0 1308 248"), attr("role", "img"), attr("aria-labelledby", "ss-rt-title ss-rt-desc")]),
        ], style="ss-realtime-diagram"),
            group([
            group([
                prose("Writer settings · PySpark 4.2+", style="ss-realtime-code-label"),
                node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                    code(""".outputMode("update")
.trigger(realTime="5 minutes")"""),
                ], style="language-python"),
            ]),
            paragraph([
                strong("Five minutes is the batch duration."),
                line_break(),
                text("It controls the progress checkpoint boundary. Records can reach the sink during that interval."),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-realtime-config"),
            paragraph([
            text("Enough task slots must be available for the running query."),
            line_break(),
            span("Supported operators and connectors depend on the Spark version and runtime."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("85"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""What if waiting for a new record to be included in the next micro-batch is too slow? Real-time mode uses long-running tasks that keep accepting records through the running pipeline. This reduces repeated batch-planning and task-launch overhead. Use a stateless Kafka-to-Kafka query here rather than implying the earlier word count is supported in every Real-time runtime.

Follow one record from read, through select or filter, to a Kafka write. These are operations, not three guaranteed Spark stages or separate executors; they may be fused into one task. The diagram is a schematic of ongoing processing, not a measured latency trace. A filter can discard a record. A record can be written before the batch ends. Micro-batch sinks can also emit while processing a batch; the distinction here is that a newly arriving input record can enter the running Real-time query without waiting for the next bounded micro-batch.

The shown PySpark settings are a fragment applied to a compatible writer before start, with Kafka and checkpointLocation configured. realTime is exposed in PySpark 4.2. The example's five minutes is the long-running batch duration used for progress checkpointing. It is not a five-minute per-record delay or a latency guarantee. Shorter durations checkpoint more often; longer durations can mean more replay after failure. Sink delivery guarantees still matter, and Kafka writes can be repeated after recovery.

Long-running execution requires enough concurrent task slots. When a supported plan has multiple pipelined stages, they need capacity together. Source, operator and sink support depends on version and runtime. The current references require Update output mode and exclude foreachBatch; output modes follow next. The Apache Real-time guide linked below is in-progress documentation and includes future version support: it says stateless support is available from 4.1, with stateful aggregation and other operators beginning in 4.3. Do not present those future sections as Apache Spark 4.2 capabilities or apply Databricks' broader operator matrix to every runtime. Continuous Processing from Spark 2.3 is a different, older experimental engine.

Transition: follow the same arriving record through micro-batch and Real-time execution on the next slide. Then return to our micro-batch word count to compare what Complete, Append and Update send to the sink.

Sources (checked 20 September 2026): PySpark 4.2 trigger API, https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.trigger.html ; released API addition, https://spark.apache.org/releases/spark-release-4-2-0.html ; Apache in-progress architecture and version notes, https://apache.github.io/spark/streaming/real-time-mode.html ; Databricks architecture, https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/real-time/concepts ; runtime-specific support, https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/real-time/reference""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_arrival_comparison() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-arrival-comparison",
        title="Micro-batch and Real-time processing",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-arrival-compare-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Micro-batch and Real-time processing", level=1),
            prose("Follow record C, arriving after processing has started.", style="ss-arrival-lead"),
            node(NodeKind.Figure, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Svg, children=[
                node(NodeKind.SvgTitle, content="When a new record can enter the running query", attributes=[attr("id", "ss-ac-title")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgDescription, content="Record C arrives at the same point in both timelines: micro-batch holds it for Batch 2 because Batch 1 has fixed input, while Real-time tasks can process C during their ongoing run.", attributes=[attr("id", "ss-ac-desc")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgDefinitions, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.SvgMarker, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgPath, attributes=[attr("d", "M0 0L8 3L0 6Z"), attr("fill", "#67747b")]),
                    ], attributes=[attr("id", "ss-ac-arrow"), attr("markerWidth", "8"), attr("markerHeight", "6"), attr("refX", "7"), attr("refY", "3"), attr("orient", "auto")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgMarker, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgPath, attributes=[attr("d", "M0 0L8 3L0 6Z"), attr("fill", "#a66714")]),
                    ], attributes=[attr("id", "ss-ac-arrow-wait"), attr("markerWidth", "8"), attr("markerHeight", "6"), attr("refX", "7"), attr("refY", "3"), attr("orient", "auto")]),
                    node(NodeKind.SvgMarker, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.SvgPath, attributes=[attr("d", "M0 0L8 3L0 6Z"), attr("fill", "#4b7c35")]),
                    ], attributes=[attr("id", "ss-ac-arrow-run"), attr("markerWidth", "8"), attr("markerHeight", "6"), attr("refX", "7"), attr("refY", "3"), attr("orient", "auto")]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Earlier", style="ss-ac-small", attributes=[attr("x", "280"), attr("y", "22")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Same arrivals · no latency scale", style="ss-ac-caption", attributes=[attr("x", "824"), attr("y", "22"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Later →", style="ss-ac-small", attributes=[attr("x", "1284"), attr("y", "22"), attr("text-anchor", "end")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgLine, style="ss-ac-rule", attributes=[attr("x1", "280"), attr("y1", "36"), attr("x2", "1284"), attr("y2", "36")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgLine, style="ss-ac-rule", attributes=[attr("x1", "616"), attr("y1", "30"), attr("x2", "616"), attr("y2", "42")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgPath, style="ss-ac-wait-arrow", attributes=[attr("d", "M672 86H956Q964 86 964 94V142")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgPath, style="ss-ac-arrow", attributes=[attr("d", "M876 184H946")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgPath, style="ss-ac-run-arrow", attributes=[attr("d", "M616 296V330")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Micro-batch", style="ss-ac-mode", attributes=[attr("x", "0"), attr("y", "178")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="New tasks per batch", style="ss-ac-small", attributes=[attr("x", "0"), attr("y", "210")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-ac-record", attributes=[attr("x", "560"), attr("y", "64"), attr("width", "112"), attr("height", "44"), attr("rx", "4")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Record C", style="ss-ac-record-text", attributes=[attr("x", "616"), attr("y", "93"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, attributes=[attr("x", "748"), attr("y", "51"), attr("width", "244"), attr("height", "28"), attr("fill", "#fafaf8")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Waits for Batch 2", style="ss-ac-wait-label", attributes=[attr("x", "870"), attr("y", "72"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-ac-batch", attributes=[attr("x", "280"), attr("y", "144"), attr("width", "596"), attr("height", "80"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Batch 1", style="ss-ac-batch-title", attributes=[attr("x", "578"), attr("y", "178"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Processes A and B", style="ss-ac-detail", attributes=[attr("x", "578"), attr("y", "208"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-ac-batch", attributes=[attr("x", "948"), attr("y", "144"), attr("width", "336"), attr("height", "80"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Batch 2", style="ss-ac-batch-title", attributes=[attr("x", "1116"), attr("y", "178"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Processes C", style="ss-ac-detail", attributes=[attr("x", "1116"), attr("y", "208"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Real-time", style="ss-ac-mode", attributes=[attr("x", "0"), attr("y", "358")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Tasks keep running", style="ss-ac-small", attributes=[attr("x", "0"), attr("y", "390")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-ac-record", attributes=[attr("x", "560"), attr("y", "252"), attr("width", "112"), attr("height", "44"), attr("rx", "4")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Record C", style="ss-ac-record-text", attributes=[attr("x", "616"), attr("y", "281"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Enters the running tasks", style="ss-ac-run-label", attributes=[attr("x", "704"), attr("y", "281")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-ac-running", attributes=[attr("x", "280"), attr("y", "332"), attr("width", "1004"), attr("height", "64"), attr("rx", "6")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="A, B…", style="ss-ac-detail", attributes=[attr("x", "360"), attr("y", "372")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgRectangle, style="ss-ac-processing", attributes=[attr("x", "616"), attr("y", "344"), attr("width", "176"), attr("height", "40"), attr("rx", "4")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="Process C", style="ss-ac-process-text", attributes=[attr("x", "704"), attr("y", "372"), attr("text-anchor", "middle")]),
                node(NodeKind.SvgText, content="…keep accepting records", style="ss-ac-detail", attributes=[attr("x", "816"), attr("y", "372")]),
            ], attributes=[attr("viewBox", "0 0 1308 416"), attr("role", "img"), attr("aria-labelledby", "ss-ac-title ss-ac-desc")]),
        ], style="ss-arrival-compare"),
            paragraph([
            text("A micro-batch fixes its input before it runs."),
            line_break(),
            span("Real-time tasks keep accepting new records during the run."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("86"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Use the same compatible stateless Kafka query from the preceding Real-time slide. A, B and C label records, not partitions or tasks. Both lanes show the same arrival history. The horizontal positions explain ordering, not benchmark timings.

Start at Batch 1. Spark has chosen a bounded slice containing A and B. C reaches Kafka after that input boundary was fixed, while Batch 1 is still running. C cannot join Batch 1; it waits at the source until a later batch includes it. In this small example that is Batch 2. Admission limits or a backlog can defer it further. The default trigger already starts the next batch as soon as the previous batch finishes; the wait drawn here is not an optional five-second timer.

Now follow the same C on the lower lane. The Real-time tasks are already running and can read the newly available record through the same stateless pipeline. They do not need a new micro-batch and a fresh task set to admit C. Processing, source polling and sink delivery still take time; spare capacity is assumed. Real-time also checkpoints progress periodically, as explained on 72. It does not eliminate every boundary or guarantee zero latency.

The distinction is input admission and task lifetime. Do not say micro-batch writes always wait until the batch ends: some sinks emit while tasks process the bounded slice. This figure also does not claim the original word-count query is supported in every Real-time runtime. Operator and connector support remains version-dependent.

Transition: we have compared how the work runs. Return to the micro-batch word count to explain which result rows the output mode sends to the sink.

Sources checked 20 September 2026: https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#triggers ; https://apache.github.io/spark/streaming/real-time-mode.html#how-real-time-mode-works (in-progress architecture documentation, not a pinned 4.2 support matrix) ; https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/real-time/concepts""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_output_modes() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-output-modes",
        title="Streaming output modes",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-output-overview",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Streaming output modes", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="The three output modes, shown for micro-batch execution", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.Columns, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Column, attributes=[attr("style", "width:240px")]),
                node(NodeKind.Column, attributes=[attr("style", "width:524px")]),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Mode", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Rows sent to the sink", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Setting on "),
                        code("writeStream"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                ]),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Complete", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("The full current result,"),
                        line_break(),
                        text("including unchanged groups."),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                            code(".outputMode(\"complete\")"),
                        ], style="language-python"),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                        text("Append"),
                        span("The default"),
                    ], attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        text("New final rows that the query"),
                        line_break(),
                        text("will not revise later."),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                            code(".outputMode(\"append\")"),
                        ], style="language-python"),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Update", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="New or updated result rows."),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                            code(".outputMode(\"update\")"),
                        ], style="language-python"),
                    ]),
                ]),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-output-mode-table"),
            paragraph([
            text("Execution mode controls how the query runs."),
            line_break(),
            span("Output mode controls which result rows it sends to the sink."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("87"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""We now return to micro-batch execution and choose what to publish. Complete, Append and Update are the three output modes. Configure one on writeStream before start; Append is the default. Our next examples keep the original Complete, Append, Update teaching order.

Complete publishes the current aggregate result. Append publishes final rows. Update publishes rows the query updated, including newly created groups. The mode selects output records; it is not the batch writer's append/overwrite save mode, nor an instruction to merge a target table. We will make the selection concrete with the same cat, dog and owl counts, then check query and sink compatibility. Real-time has separate restrictions, revisited in that compatibility check.

Definition source: https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.outputMode.html
Default and compatibility: https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#output-modes""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_complete() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-complete",
        title="Output mode: Complete",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-emission-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Output mode: Complete", level=1),
            group([
            node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                code("words.groupBy(\"word\").count()"),
            ], style="language-python"),
            node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                code(".outputMode(\"complete\")"),
            ], style="language-python"),
        ], style="ss-emission-code"),
            group([
            group([
                heading("New input", level=2),
                prose("Batch 3", style="ss-emission-label"),
                group([
                    code("dog"),
                    code("owl"),
                ], style="ss-record-list"),
            ], style="ss-emission-input"),
            group([
                text("→"),
            ], style="ss-emission-arrow", attributes=[attr("aria-hidden", "true")]),
            group([
                heading("Running counts", level=2),
                prose("Two counts change", style="ss-emission-label"),
                node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Running word counts before and after Batch 3", style="sr-only"),
                    node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="word", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Before", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Now", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, children=[
                                text("cat"),
                                span("unchanged"),
                            ], attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ], style="ss-emission-unchanged"),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="dog", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="3"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="4"),
                        ], style="ss-emission-changed"),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="owl", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="1"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ], style="ss-emission-changed"),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-emission-counts"),
            ]),
            group([
                text("→"),
            ], style="ss-emission-arrow", attributes=[attr("aria-hidden", "true")]),
            group([
                heading("Sent to the sink", level=2),
                prose("Every result row", style="ss-emission-label"),
                node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Rows emitted by Complete after Batch 3", style="sr-only"),
                    node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="word", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="count", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="cat", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="dog", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="4"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="owl", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-emission-sent"),
                prose("3 rows emitted", style="ss-emission-total"),
            ], style="ss-emission-output"),
        ], style="ss-emission-flow"),
            paragraph([
            text("Complete sends cat 2 again, even though its count did not change."),
            line_break(),
            span("All groups are retained in Complete mode."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("88"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""The same word-count query and original cat/dog/owl data as the incremental example. We zoom into Batch 3: the previous counts are cat 2, dog 3 and owl 1; the arriving records are dog and owl. Spark updates only dog and owl, producing cat 2, dog 4 and owl 2. Read from the new input through the result to the records emitted to the sink.

Complete sends all three result rows, including unchanged cat 2. It still computes incrementally; it does not reread the entire source. It retains all aggregate groups, so a watermark does not evict old groups in Complete mode. It is not a universal instruction to overwrite an arbitrary sink.

The two code blocks are query and writer-setting fragments; a compatible sink and start() are required. These are micro-batch examples and row order is illustrative. Original diagrams remain in the source backup and corpus; this version uses actual rows instead of generic document glyphs.
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.outputMode.html
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#output-modes""",
    )

