            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("98"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Original corpus: Section 8, 8.2 Spark Structured Streaming, slide 14. Keep the original destination list. Kafka does not by itself turn all end-to-end effects into exactly-once delivery. foreach is row-oriented; foreachBatch receives each micro-batch as a DataFrame and the batch ID. Its external writes need appropriate retry/idempotence handling. Memory sink supports Append and Complete, and is not fault-tolerant; console and memory are for small debugging examples. File sink supports Append. These are built-in micro-batch sink modes; operator restrictions still apply. Real-time has a different connector matrix and does not support foreachBatch. Do not treat Complete as a universal overwrite operation or Update as automatic upsert. Other table connectors can have different support. https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_start() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-start",
        title="Configuring the streaming query",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-code-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Configuring the streaming query", level=1),
            group([
            group([
                code_block("""checkpoint_path = "checkpoints/word-counts"
query = (
    counts.writeStream
    .outputMode("complete")
    .format("memory")
    .queryName("word_counts")
    .trigger(processingTime="5 seconds")
    .option("checkpointLocation", checkpoint_path)
    .start()
)""", style="language-python"),
            ]),
            group([
                heading("Return to the word-count example", level=2),
                paragraph([
                    code("writeStream"),
                    text(" configures the output."),
                ]),
                paragraph([
                    code("start()"),
                    text(" starts processing and returns a "),
                    code("StreamingQuery"),
                    text("."),
                ]),
                prose("The memory sink and local checkpoint path are for this demonstration.", style="ss-small"),
            ], style="ss-start-copy"),
        ], style="ss-start-grid"),
            group([
            heading("Read the current result", level=2),
            code_block("spark.table(\"word_counts\").orderBy(\"word\").show()", style="language-python"),
            prose("The table contains the latest completed batch’s word counts."),
        ], style="ss-result-read"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("99"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="We already started a minimal console query. Stop that run with query.stop() before starting this separate classroom demonstration. Return to the original unwindowed counts DataFrame; window_counts belonged to the separate timestamped example. Now collect the choices just explained: sink, output mode, trigger and checkpoint. Original corpus: Section 8, 8.3 recording around07:00 and11:00. Keep the writer → start → inspect result progression. queryName names the memory table in this example; it is not universally required for every query/sink. Memory output is deliberately small and is not a durable production destination. Configure checkpointLocation before start(). This local path persists progress and aggregation state for the demonstration; in a cluster, choose durable shared storage accessible after application replacement. The following slides explain what is saved and how a compatible query resumes. In Complete mode, a restarted memory query can recreate its full result table, but that does not make driver memory durable. The five-second trigger is an interval, not a promise to finish every five seconds. Use separate arriving files to reproduce the three moments; placing all files in the input directory before start may process them together. A driver-side spark.table read inspects results, not execution metrics. Use query.lastProgress for progress. https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_checkpointing() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-checkpointing",
        title="Checkpointing a streaming query",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-checkpoint-overview",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Checkpointing a streaming query", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
            code(".option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"checkpoints/word-counts\")"),
        ], style="language-python ss-checkpoint-option"),
            paragraph([
            text("Set this on "),
            code("writeStream"),
            text(" before "),
            code("start()"),
            text(". Local demonstration path shown."),
        ], style="ss-checkpoint-code-note"),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="The information Spark retains for recovery", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="What Spark saves", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Why it matters after a restart", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                ]),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Source progress", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Recover the input range that needs processing."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Completed batches", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Know which work is already committed."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Operator state", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Restore the counts or other state the query needs next."),
                ]),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-checkpoint-contents"),
            paragraph([
            text("Use durable storage, with one checkpoint location per query."),
            line_break(),
            span("Restart with that same location and a compatible query."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("100"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Connect the explicit checkpointLocation on the previous writer example to the word-count state the audience already knows. A checkpoint directory contains input progress logs, completed-batch records, operator state for stateful queries, and query metadata such as query identity. Those pieces work together for recovery. It is not a copy of every source record or a backup of the destination, nor a serialized Python process. Stateless queries still need progress tracking. This is Structured Streaming query checkpointing, distinct from DataFrame.checkpoint() for truncating a batch execution plan and from cache/persist.

Use durable, supported shared storage in a cluster. The relative path is only for the local classroom example. Keep it stable across restarts and exclusive to one logical query; never run two active queries against it. A new or deleted checkpoint means a fresh query, which can replay input according to the source's starting rules while old sink output remains. Do not teach checkpoint deletion as a routine fix. Keep required source data available for recovery.

Reusing a checkpoint requires a compatible query. Changing grouping keys, aggregates, state schemas, sources or sinks can require a migration or a new checkpoint; supported changes depend on the release and connector. The checkpoint does not deploy or restart the application for you. Relaunch the application and reconstruct the compatible query with the same location. Real-time mode also checkpoints progress periodically; the next example specifically follows micro-batch recovery.

https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#recovering-from-failures-with-checkpointing
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#recovery-semantics-after-changes-in-a-streaming-query
https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/checkpoints""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_checkpoint_recovery() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-checkpoint-recovery",
        title="Restarting from a checkpoint",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-checkpoint-recovery",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Restarting from a checkpoint", level=1),
            prose("The same word count · micro-batch execution", style="ss-checkpoint-scope"),
            group([
            group([
                heading("Saved after Batch 2", level=2),
                prose("Last committed state", style="ss-recovery-label"),
                node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Word counts saved after Batch 2", style="sr-only"),
                    node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="word", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="count", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="cat", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="dog", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="3"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="owl", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="1"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-recovery-state"),
                prose("Files 01.txt and 02.txt processed", style="ss-recovery-input-note"),
            ], style="ss-recovery-saved"),
            group([
                text("→"),
            ], style="ss-recovery-arrow", attributes=[attr("aria-hidden", "true")]),
            group([
                heading("Batch 3 interrupted", level=2),
                prose("Commit not recorded", style="ss-recovery-label"),
                group([
                    code("dog"),
                    code("owl"),
                ], style="ss-recovery-records"),
                prose("Input from 03.txt", style="ss-recovery-input-note"),
            ], style="ss-recovery-interrupted"),
            group([
                text("→"),
            ], style="ss-recovery-arrow", attributes=[attr("aria-hidden", "true")]),
            group([
                heading("Restart and replay Batch 3", level=2),
                prose("Reuse the same checkpoint", style="ss-recovery-label"),
                node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Recovered counts and counts after replaying Batch 3", style="sr-only"),
                    node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="word", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Restored", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="After replay", attributes=[attr("scope", "col")]),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="cat", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="dog", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="3"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="4"),
                        ]),
                        node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="owl", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="1"),
                            node(NodeKind.Cell, content="2"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ], style="ss-recovery-result"),
                prose("Continue from the recovered counts", style="ss-recovery-input-note"),
            ], style="ss-recovery-resumed"),
        ], style="ss-recovery-flow"),
            paragraph([
            text("An unfinished batch can run again."),
            line_break(),
            span("The source must support replay, and the sink must handle retries correctly."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("101"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""A concrete failure scenario using the same prepared word-count files. Batch 2 has committed. Its input progress and state are recoverable: cat 2, dog 3, owl 1. Batch 3 selects file 03.txt, containing dog and owl, but the application is interrupted before completion is recorded. On restart with the same compatible query and checkpoint, Spark recovers the committed state and replays the unfinished batch. The recovered state plus that input produces cat 2, dog 4, owl 2. It does not start the counts at zero or add the same failed batch twice to the committed aggregation. The displayed batch numbers are teaching labels; actual batch IDs are normally zero-based.

This is an ordering example, not a time scale or a promise that recovery is instant. Internally, offset, commit and state-store logs participate in recovery; partially written files are not equivalent to a committed batch. New executors can load saved state. Keep the required source data available: checkpointing cannot restore Kafka records that retention removed or input files that were deleted.

The sink caveat matters. If output was written before the failure but completion was not recorded, a retry may write again. Exactly-once external effects require a compatible replayable source and a sink with the necessary transactional or idempotent behaviour; checkpointing alone is not a universal exactly-once guarantee. Kafka output is at-least-once, and custom foreachBatch writers need their own retry/deduplication design. The classroom memory sink is for inspecting small results; in Complete mode it can recreate its full table after restarting, but it is not a durable external destination. A normal stop/restart is also supported, without needing to simulate a crash.

https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#recovering-from-failures-with-checkpointing
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/getting-started.html#fault-tolerance-semantics
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html#output-sinks""",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_operation() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-operation",
        title="Managing a streaming query",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Managing a streaming query", level=1),
            group([
            group([
                code("lines.isStreaming"),
                prose("Is this an unbounded DataFrame?"),
            ]),
            group([
                code("query.isActive"),
                prose("Is the query still running?"),
            ]),
            group([
                code("query.lastProgress"),
                prose("What happened in the most recent trigger?"),
            ]),
            group([
                code("query.stop()"),
                prose("Stop this running query."),
            ]),
            group([
                code("query.awaitTermination()"),
                prose("Keep the application waiting while it runs."),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-management"),
            paragraph([
            text("Keep the query handle returned by "),
            code("start()"),
            text("."),
        ], style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("102"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Original corpus: Section 8, 8.3 Managing and Converting Streams, recording around11:00. In PySpark, isStreaming, isActive and lastProgress are properties; stop and awaitTermination are methods. lastProgress can be None before a progress report is available. These are independent controls, not a script to execute sequentially: awaitTermination is normally used instead of immediately stopping. Stop ends this run; it does not mean the stream has a natural end. A compatible restart uses the same checkpoint location, as just shown. Application deployment and production supervision remain later operational topics. https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html API reference: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/query_management.html",
    )

pub def slide_streaming_recap() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="streaming-recap",
        title="Structured Streaming: recap",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide streaming-slide ss-chapter-recap ss-with-time",
        content=[
            group([
            text("07 / STRUCTURED STREAMING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Structured Streaming: recap", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="The main ideas from the Structured Streaming chapter", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.Columns, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Column, style="ss-summary-topic"),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="DataFrame API", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        paragraph([
                            code("readStream"),
                            text(" and transformations describe the query."),
                        ]),
                        paragraph([
                            code("writeStream.start()"),
                            text(" begins processing and returns the running-query handle."),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Execution", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        paragraph([
                            strong("Micro-batch:"),
                            text(" each batch processes a fixed set of input."),
                        ]),
                        paragraph([
                            strong("Real-time:"),
                            text(" running tasks can accept new records as they arrive."),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Output", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        group([
                            group([
                                strong("Append"),
                                span("New, final rows"),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                strong("Update"),
                                span("Changed rows"),
                            ]),
                            group([
                                strong("Complete"),
                                span("The whole result"),
                            ]),
                        ], style="ss-summary-modes"),
                        prose("The sink determines where those rows go.", style="ss-summary-sink"),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="State & time", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        prose("State carries results between batches. Event time places records in windows."),
                        prose("A watermark lets eligible old windows close, trading lateness tolerance for retained state."),
                    ]),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Recovery", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, children=[
                        prose("Reuse a durable checkpoint to restore progress and state on a compatible restart."),
                        prose("Unfinished work may be replayed; the sink must handle retries."),
                    ]),
                ]),
            ]),
        ], style="ss-chapter-summary"),
            prose("The query, execution mode, output mode and sink must be compatible.", style="ss-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("103"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""We began with familiar DataFrame code. A growing input makes execution, emitted results and recovery explicit choices. Recall record C: a micro-batch fixes its input; Real-time tasks can accept later arrivals. Keep runtime-specific support qualified. Output mode selects rows; it does not guarantee delivery. Restart requires the same checkpoint and a compatible query. Unfinished work can replay. Use the handle returned by start() to monitor lastProgress or stop the run. Recall the late12:03 cat: arrival order does not determine its window. Under Append, finalization and state removal wait for watermark progress. Complete preserves aggregate groups. The delay is measured against event time, not wall-clock waiting. State-store implementation details remain outside this introduction.

https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/streaming/apis-on-dataframes-and-datasets.html
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.trigger.html
https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/real-time/concepts""",
    )

